    <h1>Semaglutide: analytical profile for research buyers</h1>
    <p class="lead muted">A modified GLP-1 analogue whose side chain changes the analytical picture. This profile covers what to verify, not what the compound does.</p>
    <p class="small muted" style="margin-bottom:18px;">Part of the <a href="/knowledge/glp-1-research-peptides-framework/">GLP-1 research peptides pillar</a>.</p>

    <h2>What the molecule is</h2>
    <p>Semaglutide is a 31-amino-acid GLP-1 analogue with two design features that matter analytically: a fatty-acid side chain attached through a linker, and sequence modifications that slow enzymatic degradation. The side chain supports albumin binding and is the reason the molecule behaves differently on reverse-phase HPLC than an unmodified GLP-1 sequence.</p>
    <p>Reference molecular weight: ≈4113.6 g/mol for the parent compound. Salt form and counter-ion change the mass of the actual material, so the MS interpretation must match the form you bought — acetate and TFA salts are not interchangeable in a mass calculation.</p>

    <h2>Why the side chain matters for HPLC</h2>
    <p>The fatty-acid moiety increases hydrophobicity and shifts retention. Consequences:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>A generic GLP-1 gradient may elute semaglutide later than expected, or with poor peak shape.</li>
      <li>Side-chain-related impurities (incomplete acylation, linker fragments) can elute close to the parent.</li>
      <li>Method transfer between suppliers is not automatic — the column and gradient should be stated on the COA.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>When reviewing a chromatogram, check that the method is described well enough to reproduce, and that the integration shows the impurity profile rather than a single peak with no baseline detail.</p>

    <h2>Mass spectrometry expectations</h2>
    <p>MS confirmation should match the modified sequence, not the unmodified backbone. A common failure pattern in the market is a COA whose observed mass corresponds to a different analogue or to a truncated sequence. Ask for the spectrum and the expected mass calculation, and check the adduct pattern — sodium and potassium adducts are normal, but they should be interpretable.</p>

    <h2>Solubility and handling</h2>
    <p>The modified molecule is less hydrophilic than the native sequence. Practical notes:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Dissolve slowly; add solvent down the vial wall and allow time.</li>
      <li>If aqueous solubility is limiting, follow the COA's recommended solvent rather than improvising.</li>
      <li>Avoid vortexing — hydrophobic sequences are more prone to interfacial aggregation.</li>
      <li>Use low-binding tubes at low concentrations to limit adsorption loss.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Counter-ion and net content</h2>
    <p>Semaglutide is commonly supplied as the acetate salt. The counter-ion fraction is not peptide, and it is not visible in an HPLC purity number. For cost-per-mg comparisons, ask for water content and net peptide content; without them, two vials at the same nominal fill can differ materially in what you actually receive.</p>

    <h2>Verification checklist</h2>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>What to look for</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Identity</td><td>MS spectrum matching the modified sequence and the correct salt form</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Purity</td><td>HPLC chromatogram with method, gradient and integration</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Water content</td><td>Karl Fischer value stated</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Net peptide</td><td>Assay or content value, not just purity</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Batch linkage</td><td>Lot number on vial, COA and invoice</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Consistency</td><td>Consecutive lot data if reordering</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2>Editor's take</h2>
    <p>Semaglutide's side chain is not a detail; it is the analytical problem. Buyers who ignore it compare chromatograms that were never measuring the same thing.</p>

    <h2>Limitations</h2>
    <p>Molecular and analytical information only; no dosing, efficacy or human-use guidance. The batch COA governs the material supplied.</p>

